"Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/832"} "Guojia Tiyuguan, Beijing Aolinpike Gongyuan, Beijing"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/259991"} "15 from 15 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The format was the same as it had been from 1984-2004, with eight gymnasts advancing from the team all-around, but no more than two per nation. The favorite was China’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/107151"} "Cheng Fei"] " who had won the 2005-07 World Championships on vault. She led the qualifying and her first vault scored 16.075 which led American " [:a {:href "/athletes/117652"} "Alicia Sacramone"] ", who had won medals at those three Worlds, silver in 2006 and bronze in 2005/07, by 0.325 points. However, Cheng lost her balance on her second vault and fell to her knees, dropping down to a bronze medal eventually. The gold went to North Korean " [:a {:href "/athletes/117720"} "Hong Un-Jong"] " who posted the highest score in the second round, with 15.750. Sacramone had two steady vaults, but neither spectacular, and missed the podium in fourth place."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Hong led Germany’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/29304"} "Oksana Chusovitina"] ", who at 33-years-old, was easily the oldest gymnast in Beijing. Chusovitina set a record in 2008 by becoming the first female gymnast to compete at five Olympics, but this was her first for Germany. She had first competed in 1992 as a member of the Unified Team, and then represented her native Uzbekistan from 1996-2004. Chusovitina later settled in Germany while seeking medical treatment for her son Alisher, who had leukemia. She would also compete in 2012, in a record sixth Olympics."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "454"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/117720"} "Hong Un-Jong"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "